WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:10.420
- I'm really delighted today
to introduce Ting Hui Lau

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:12.900
who is a newly appointed
Assistant Professor

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.453
of Anthropology at Yale-NUS College.

00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:20.650
And I consider Ting to be
a friend and colleague.

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:23.740
I got to know Ting a
little bit during my year,

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:26.060
the tail end of my year in Singapore

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:29.063
when I was teaching as
visiting faculty at Yale-NUS,

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:33.380
before she had started her position there.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:35.170
And so I'm really excited to know

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:37.070
that she's taking up the position there

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:39.420
because I think she's
gonna do important things

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:42.190
to complement the already strong

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:43.793
anthropology department there.

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:49.350
She recently received her PhD
in Anthropology from Cornell,

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:52.140
and she is already in
the process of finalizing

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.260
a book manuscript entitled,

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:57.181
Wounds of Progress Colonial Development

00:00:57.181 --> 00:01:02.181
and the Politics of Affliction
on the China-Myanmar Border.

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:05.720
And the dissertation fieldwork
which informs the book

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:08.160
is built on a decade of engagement

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.360
and two years of continuous field work

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:13.430
with Lisu subsistence farmers.

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:17.080
And Ting has earned a reputation
in Southeast Asian studies

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.700
for her tenacity as a field worker

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:23.340
in notoriously difficult
field environment.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.410
Colleagues I know from Cornell

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:27.520
and others who know about her work

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.660
constantly rave about the
importance of her project,

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.860
which rethinks the violent colonial

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:34.760
dimensions of development,

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:38.678
which in this area, instead of
delivering poverty reduction

00:01:38.678 --> 00:01:42.630
has inadvertently perpetuated
high rates of violence,

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:46.650
alcoholism, disease and mental illness,

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:49.190
which are interpreted by the Lisu's forms

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:52.683
of haunting, curses in demon madness.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:55.820
Really important and fascinating work

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:58.590
by a really important rising new scholar

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:00.280
in Southeast Asian Studies.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:02.397
So please join me in welcoming Ting

00:02:02.397 --> 00:02:04.490
for the talk today entitled,

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.180
Conjuring Spirits:

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:09.840
The Melancholic Play of
Alcohol Drinking Lisu Men

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.423
on The China-Myanmar Border.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:14.370
- Good afternoon, everyone.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:16.270
And thank you so much, Eric,

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:18.742
for the very kind introduction

00:02:18.742 --> 00:02:20.750
and thank you everybody for being here.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:23.620
It's such a great honor to
have a chance to share my work

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:27.180
with the Southeast Asian
Studies Community in Yale.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:30.700
And I'm really extremely
grateful for the opportunity.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:33.390
Today, I will be sharing
a chapter from my book

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:35.399
in Progress, Means of Progress,

00:02:35.399 --> 00:02:38.943
Colonial Development and
The Politics of Affliction.

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.590
The book examines development

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:45.390
and affliction among indigenous Lisu men

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:49.003
in the Nu River Valley on
the China-Myanmar border.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:53.540
Following a group of Lisu
men in their struggle

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:57.520
with chronic pain, alcoholism and madness,

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.212
I examined the social and political forces

00:03:00.212 --> 00:03:03.120
that give rise to these afflictions

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:06.333
as well as the ways that
Lisu regenerate from them.

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:11.150
The chapter I will be talking about today,

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:13.200
focuses on alcohol drinking

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.015
and what leads to term, alcohol madness.

00:03:16.015 --> 00:03:18.560
(mumbles)

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:19.883
Do you see my next slide?

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:23.940
Alcohol drinking gatherings are common

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:27.100
across these two villages
in the Nu River Valley.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:30.610
These gatherings last between
a few hours and a week

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:34.350
during which the men do
not work and hardly eat,

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:36.960
spending their nights and days drinking,

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:40.290
role-playing, telling
stories, and singing.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.660
They descend into alcohol madness.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:44.440
When thus possessed,

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:48.260
the men are no longer
authors of their own words,

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:51.653
but speak on behalf of the spirits, Ni.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:55.690
Straddling the space between
the living and the dead,

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:58.300
the alcohol mad men haunt villagers

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:00.418
with their (mumbles) laughter,

00:04:00.418 --> 00:04:03.010
maniacal speeches, and uncanny cries

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:05.833
for the lost souls of forgotten ancestors.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:10.010
This paper examines alcohol drinking

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:12.250
as a form of melancholic play

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:14.330
through which non-Christian Lisu men,

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:17.310
refuse indigenous cultural erasure

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:21.702
and the Chinese development
and Christian missionization.

00:04:21.702 --> 00:04:22.960
Lisu in the Valley,

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:26.410
have long had to grapple
with the dual colonial forces

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:30.990
of Christian missionization
and Chinese State encroachment.

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:33.870
Christianity was introduced
by foreign missionaries

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.210
in the early 20th Century

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:38.140
or Chinese State encroachment

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:40.880
has been ongoing for centuries,

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.363
accelerating since the 1950s.

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:46.770
Both these ideologies of progress

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:49.500
consider indigenous Lisu ritual practices,

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:51.360
such as alcohol drinking,

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.890
as backward and uncivilized.

00:04:54.890 --> 00:04:58.937
These propagandas (mumbles)
a static image of the Lisu

00:04:58.937 --> 00:05:03.123
and contribute to Lisu
cultural loss and dignity loss.

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:07.890
I show how non-Christian
alcohol drinking men

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:09.943
refused such ideologies.

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:13.920
Gathering to drink and play,

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:18.920
the men infuse melancholia
with play humor and laughter

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:23.290
and keep supposedly bygone practices alive

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:25.440
through the partial revitalization

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:27.923
of old alcohol drinking rituals.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:33.590
This analysis joins scholar
limitizing Eurocentric ideas

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:35.950
about melancholia as pathological.

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:37.600
So the conventional idea,

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:39.000
the conventional Freudian idea,

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:42.286
that melancholia is a incomplete grief,

00:05:42.286 --> 00:05:44.440
grief that is unhealthy

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:46.923
because the mourning
can never be concluded.

00:05:48.256 --> 00:05:50.930
Situating Lisu experiences of loss

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:53.700
in relation to historical colonialism,

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:56.327
my analysis shows how the pathology lies

00:05:56.327 --> 00:05:59.880
not in Lisu relationships with loss,

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:03.890
but in the colonial
processes undergirding loss.

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:05.080
In this context,

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:10.080
melancholia is not a passive
inability to embrace change,

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:13.283
but an active unwillingness to move on.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:18.360
The talk is organized into four parts.

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:20.290
I will begin with a brief introduction

00:06:20.290 --> 00:06:21.680
of the colonial dynamics

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:24.890
animating the ethnographic
context in which I work.

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:26.530
Next, I discuss alcohol

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:29.571
within the triad of Lisu
indigenous cultural paradigms,

00:06:29.571 --> 00:06:32.524
Christianity and Chinese development

00:06:32.524 --> 00:06:35.260
following which I delve into a night

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:37.610
of drinking and melancholic play

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:40.740
with a group of non-Christian
alcohol drinking men.

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:42.489
And finally, I close this talk

00:06:42.489 --> 00:06:45.930
with a brief discussion
on listening to affliction

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:48.900
and the implications of
such forms of listening

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:50.500
for public health interventions.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:55.220
So I'm gonna begin with a brief discussion

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:57.663
on the ethnographic
context to set the scene.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:03.860
The Lisu are an indigenous
people living dispersed

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:06.483
across the Highlands of
mainland Southeast Asia,

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:11.920
along the borders of China,
Myanmar, Thailand, and India.

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:16.240
My ethnography focuses on the
Lisu, in the Nu River Valley

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:18.290
on the China-Myanmar border.

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:21.463
As you can see the river
follows the blue line there.

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:24.590
The Valley is formally within the borders

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:25.840
of the Chinese State,

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:27.340
but geographically located

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:29.740
in the Highlands of
mainland Southeast Asia,

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:33.140
the region referred to by
some scholars as Zomia.

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:36.700
Scholars have defined Zomia
as a vast (mumbles) zone

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:38.537
where communities have historically fled

00:07:38.537 --> 00:07:41.060
to escape state rule.

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:43.970
The Lisu are one of the
many indigenous communities

00:07:43.970 --> 00:07:46.373
residing in this trans border terrain.

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:53.740
The Nu River Valley

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:56.470
is a traditional territory
of many indigenous peoples

00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:59.370
with the Lisu constituting one
of the largest communities.

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:02.232
Like many countries in Asia,

00:08:02.232 --> 00:08:04.830
China does not recognize the existence

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:07.233
of indigenous groups within its borders.

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:09.390
The Lisu are classified

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:11.770
by the Chinese communist government,

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:15.803
as one of the officially
recognized 55 ethnic minorities.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:19.820
But the Lisu experience
of cultural erasure

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:21.910
and political marginalization,

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:25.160
under Chinese assimilationist,
ethnic policies,

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:26.920
resonates with the struggles

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:29.620
of indigenous communities elsewhere.

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:32.000
As (mumbles) argue,

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:36.080
it is the oppositional
please base existence

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:39.310
along with the consciousness
of being in struggle

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:40.880
against the dispossessing

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:45.310
and demeaning fact of
colonization by foreign peoples

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:49.310
that fundamentally
distinguishes indigenous peoples

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:51.970
from other peoples of the world.

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:54.630
This article connects the Lisu struggle

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:57.850
with the experiences of
indigenous communities elsewhere

00:08:57.850 --> 00:09:00.980
to relativize Chinese
ethnic classifications,

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:04.960
to underscore the psychic
and cultural experience

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:06.120
of being Lisu.

00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:10.670
Like other indigenous communities

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:13.930
residing in this trans
border Highland region,

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:17.160
the Lisu wrestle with
multiple colonial forces,

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:19.950
including state civilizational projects,

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:24.800
economic development and
religious missionization.

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:27.210
These processes have estranged Lisu

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:29.380
from the traditional ritual practices

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:32.478
which they call practices
of the earlier days.

00:09:32.478 --> 00:09:35.110
(speaking foreign language)

00:09:35.110 --> 00:09:38.900
These include making offerings
to the spirits, (mumbles)

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:42.773
soul calling (mumbles)
spirit dancing, (mumbles)

00:09:42.773 --> 00:09:47.100
and sorcery, (mumbles) and
reassemble those carried out

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:49.563
by other indigenous
communities in the region.

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:54.040
When I began my dissertation
fieldwork in 2015,

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:57.320
I came eager to learn
about Lisu ritual life,

00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:00.310
but many of my Lisu interlocutors
treated my questions

00:10:00.310 --> 00:10:03.620
about rituals as inquiries about the past.

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:05.094
They explained that these rituals,

00:10:05.094 --> 00:10:09.080
which often involved alcohol
were pre-Christian practices

00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:12.450
that were no longer being followed.

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:15.070
As with many Christianized
indigenous communities,

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:16.770
the introduction of Christianity

00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:20.700
effectively divided history
into two distinct parts;

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:23.453
pre Christianity and post Christianity.

00:10:25.790 --> 00:10:29.250
Today, the majority of
the Lisu in the Valley

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:32.890
practice indigenized
protestant Christianity.

00:10:32.890 --> 00:10:34.310
All Christian activities

00:10:34.310 --> 00:10:36.470
must be endorsed by the central church,

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:38.210
which is part of the state sanction,

00:10:38.210 --> 00:10:40.043
Three Self Protesting Church.

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:43.370
Although some Lisu practice alternative

00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:46.210
non-state sanctioned
forms of Christianity,

00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:49.250
they are actively persecuted
by the Chinese communist party

00:10:49.250 --> 00:10:50.703
and have become a minority.

00:10:51.650 --> 00:10:54.379
Despite government's surveillance
of Christian activities,

00:10:54.379 --> 00:10:57.840
many Christian Lisu continue
to relate to Christianity

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:00.393
as something beyond
full government control.

00:11:01.848 --> 00:11:05.450
Christianity remains an
important source of power,

00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:10.450
healing and resistance against
Chinese state (mumbles)

00:11:10.572 --> 00:11:13.240
But dominantly Lisu Christian ideology

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:15.130
also have many structural parallels

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:17.112
with Chinese ideologies of development

00:11:17.112 --> 00:11:20.920
including a linear notion
of time and progress.

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:22.190
Christian needs to reproduce

00:11:22.190 --> 00:11:24.420
Chinese domination in many ways,

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:26.900
for example, Lisu Christians,

00:11:26.900 --> 00:11:29.318
a lineal conception of time and progress,

00:11:29.318 --> 00:11:34.030
denigrate non-Christians as
primitive demon worshipers.

00:11:34.030 --> 00:11:36.800
Although many Lisu Christian practices

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:39.150
are similar to traditional rituals,

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:40.362
Christian Lisu work hard

00:11:40.362 --> 00:11:44.003
to differentiate themselves
from non-Christians.

00:11:44.003 --> 00:11:47.510
Lisu Christianity
forbids smoking, gambling

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:51.894
telling non-Christian once
upon a time origin stories

00:11:51.894 --> 00:11:55.403
(mumbles) and above all drinking alcohol.

00:11:57.290 --> 00:12:00.070
Many Christian Lisu hesitate
even to share memories

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:02.510
of traditional cosmologies and rituals

00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:05.380
for fear of being labeled unfaithful

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:08.023
and being ostracized by
the Christian community.

00:12:09.650 --> 00:12:11.440
Certainly, Christian Lisu

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:13.440
have a complex and contextual relationship

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:16.450
with non-Christian Lisu
practices and people,

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:19.560
but a defining characteristic
of many Christian Lisu

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:23.860
is the constant cultural work
they do to distance themselves

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:26.903
from their supposedly simple
non-Christian Lisu past.

00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:36.750
The rise of Christianity has occurred

00:12:36.750 --> 00:12:39.550
alongside rapid Chinese development.

00:12:39.550 --> 00:12:42.460
Like many ethnic communities
and contemporary China,

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:45.440
Lisu have negotiated with
the civilizing mission

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:48.383
of encroaching Chinese
state power for centuries.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:51.154
In the communist era,

00:12:51.154 --> 00:12:54.340
rapid Chinese development
has further upended the ways

00:12:54.340 --> 00:12:57.010
of life of the Lisu and other minorities

00:12:57.010 --> 00:13:00.143
with accelerating
upheaval since the 2000s.

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:04.940
Like Christianity, Chinese
development discourses

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:08.553
deny the (mumbles) of Lisu
indigenous life worlds.

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:11.481
While Christianity is recognized

00:13:11.481 --> 00:13:14.270
by many Lisu and Chinese people

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:16.110
as a modern foreign religion,

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:20.830
Lisu indigenous ritual practices
are labeled as primitive.

00:13:20.830 --> 00:13:24.373
Many Lisu have internalized
this ideology of contempt.

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:26.963
Through residential schooling,

00:13:26.963 --> 00:13:29.650
factory work and urbanization,

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:31.150
Lisu have being recruited

00:13:31.150 --> 00:13:33.320
into the Chinese state bureaucracy,

00:13:33.320 --> 00:13:37.290
market economy and Han majority society.

00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:39.030
These assimilationist programs

00:13:39.030 --> 00:13:41.533
have gained intensity in recent years.

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:47.130
Since 2011, Chinese ethnic policies

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:49.610
have adopted a fiercer
assimilationist stance

00:13:49.610 --> 00:13:51.903
towards its ethnic minority populations.

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:54.320
In this context,

00:13:54.320 --> 00:13:58.180
the arenas in which minority
ways of life can be recognized,

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:00.196
I've narrowed to the domains of tourism

00:14:00.196 --> 00:14:02.620
or ethnological research,

00:14:02.620 --> 00:14:05.140
that is, Lisu traditional practices

00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:07.804
are being petrified into static objects

00:14:07.804 --> 00:14:11.241
for tourists and research consumption.

00:14:11.241 --> 00:14:14.410
Lisu are thus simultaneously
being assimilated

00:14:14.410 --> 00:14:16.990
into the dominant Chinese society

00:14:16.990 --> 00:14:19.943
and being objectified into artifacts.

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:25.280
So my next section talks about alcohol

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:28.530
and how alcohol works within this context.

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:30.860
I titled this The Gift of Alcohol

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:32.710
and it's the gift from the ancestors.

00:14:36.950 --> 00:14:39.417
Alcohol lies at the center
of the triadic relationship

00:14:39.417 --> 00:14:42.261
between these two indigenous
cultural paradigms,

00:14:42.261 --> 00:14:45.083
Christianity, and Chinese development.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:49.070
Once a sacred item used
during important ritual,

00:14:49.070 --> 00:14:53.350
alcohol was respected as a
powerful substance by the Lisu.

00:14:54.270 --> 00:14:56.710
Traditionally, among many Lisu,

00:14:56.710 --> 00:14:59.640
alcohol was seen as a
gift from the spirits,

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:04.080
a powerful medium that
connects humans to non-humans

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:06.663
and transform sadness to joy.

00:15:07.510 --> 00:15:10.980
Accepting the gift of
alcohol, binds the Lisu person

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:14.100
to a reciprocal relationship
with the spirits.

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:16.270
Lisu sorceress, (mumbles)

00:15:16.270 --> 00:15:19.430
were people who knew how to
wheel the power of alcohol

00:15:19.430 --> 00:15:21.203
to communicate with spirits.

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:26.880
My Lisu interlocutors described
how sorceress used alcohol

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:29.280
as means to summon the spirits.

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:31.470
With the aid of the assembled spirits,

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:34.280
these sorceress (mumbles)
deserving punishment,

00:15:34.280 --> 00:15:37.660
and sort justice for
those who were wronged.

00:15:37.660 --> 00:15:41.740
Alcohol was used in healing
rituals, for example,

00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:45.370
it was also using feeling
rituals, for example,

00:15:45.370 --> 00:15:48.490
if a soul left the body after
a frightening encounter,

00:15:48.490 --> 00:15:52.380
skillful sorceress could
sometimes reunite the lost souls

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:55.693
with their host bodies through
alcohol mediated rituals.

00:15:57.160 --> 00:16:00.520
But for those who were less skillful,

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:02.283
alcohol could be overpowering,

00:16:03.150 --> 00:16:05.020
it could deceive.

00:16:05.020 --> 00:16:08.740
If a person was unable to control
the voices of the spirits,

00:16:08.740 --> 00:16:10.460
alcohol consumption can lead

00:16:10.460 --> 00:16:13.970
to temporary or permanent
madness (mumbles)

00:16:13.970 --> 00:16:17.470
or spirit induce epilepsy. (mumbles)

00:16:17.470 --> 00:16:20.530
Out of respect for the powerful spirits,

00:16:20.530 --> 00:16:24.270
Lisu made offerings of alcohol
and meat to the spirits,

00:16:24.270 --> 00:16:26.443
to honor their reciprocal ties.

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:31.300
But as indigenous Lisu ritual paradigms

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:34.480
and practices eroded with
conversion to Christianity,

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:36.360
and the Chinese development,

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:38.733
so did the status of alcohol.

00:16:40.181 --> 00:16:42.640
The sacred relationship with alcohol

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:44.210
has changed significantly

00:16:44.210 --> 00:16:46.963
with development and
conversion to Christianity.

00:16:48.130 --> 00:16:51.130
Alcohol is now rejected by Christians

00:16:51.130 --> 00:16:54.527
as a poison that tempts and contaminates.

00:16:54.527 --> 00:16:57.490
The (mumbles) speech of drunk men,

00:16:57.490 --> 00:17:00.040
are the words of the demons within them

00:17:00.040 --> 00:17:02.793
rather than the human animating the body.

00:17:03.670 --> 00:17:04.584
The alcohol drinkers

00:17:04.584 --> 00:17:07.890
are no longer seen as skillful sorcerers,

00:17:07.890 --> 00:17:09.256
but as useless drunkards

00:17:09.256 --> 00:17:13.130
or worse yet Satan's underlings.

00:17:13.130 --> 00:17:16.976
Christian Lisu instead
accept another kind of gift,

00:17:16.976 --> 00:17:19.795
(mumbles) the gift of eternal life,

00:17:19.795 --> 00:17:23.080
(mumbles) from God, (mumbles)

00:17:23.080 --> 00:17:24.860
Embracing God's grace,

00:17:24.860 --> 00:17:26.173
they seek to break away

00:17:26.173 --> 00:17:30.603
from their supposedly demonic
past which alcohol recalls.

00:17:32.030 --> 00:17:34.790
Similarly, Chinese development discourses

00:17:34.790 --> 00:17:38.020
often portray minority
people, like the Lisu,

00:17:38.020 --> 00:17:41.580
as people who drink too
much and who are ungrateful.

00:17:41.580 --> 00:17:44.446
Under Chinese and Christian
ideologies of progress,

00:17:44.446 --> 00:17:47.150
alcohol drinking Lisu are referred

00:17:47.150 --> 00:17:49.290
as primitive or damaged people

00:17:49.290 --> 00:17:51.706
and their spirits and the
spirits they mingle with

00:17:51.706 --> 00:17:53.700
are branded as demonic

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:57.040
or dismissed as superstitious delusions.

00:17:57.040 --> 00:17:59.670
Hypocritically, the Han majority

00:17:59.670 --> 00:18:03.102
sees mainstream drinking and
Han society as masculine,

00:18:03.102 --> 00:18:05.523
violent, productive and civilized.

00:18:07.490 --> 00:18:10.230
These days, alcohol
drinking Lisu men and women

00:18:10.230 --> 00:18:13.201
are stigmatized by Christian
and Chinese discourses

00:18:13.201 --> 00:18:16.080
that often portrayed them as primitive,

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:20.190
sexually loose, poor, smelly and dirty.

00:18:20.190 --> 00:18:22.370
These discourses are gendered.

00:18:22.370 --> 00:18:24.350
Although many Lisu women drink,

00:18:24.350 --> 00:18:27.160
men are usually the heavy drinkers.

00:18:27.160 --> 00:18:29.560
Alcohol drinking men, (mumbles)

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:33.460
has become a general term
of the used derogatorily.

00:18:33.460 --> 00:18:34.740
I'm very happy to talk more

00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:37.468
about the gender dynamics in the Q and A.

00:18:37.468 --> 00:18:40.630
This talk focuses, and my book in Progress

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:42.910
generally focuses on men,

00:18:42.910 --> 00:18:45.310
but I actually spent most
of my time with women.

00:18:47.050 --> 00:18:49.820
But contrary to popular
and Christian beliefs,

00:18:49.820 --> 00:18:52.750
alcohol dependency and
alcohol related violence

00:18:52.750 --> 00:18:54.910
emerged relatively recently.

00:18:54.910 --> 00:18:58.640
Traditionally Lisu
alcohol is made from maize

00:18:58.640 --> 00:19:00.790
because maize is an important staple food

00:19:00.790 --> 00:19:02.560
for humans and animals.

00:19:02.560 --> 00:19:04.857
In former times only social elites

00:19:04.857 --> 00:19:07.040
with an abundance of maize

00:19:07.040 --> 00:19:09.193
had the resources to make alcohol.

00:19:10.830 --> 00:19:13.140
The expansion of commercial alcohol

00:19:14.220 --> 00:19:16.950
transform Lisu relationships with it,

00:19:16.950 --> 00:19:18.640
As commercial beer and liquor

00:19:18.640 --> 00:19:22.160
became widely and cheaply
available in stores

00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:26.090
and real estate commissaries
in the 1980s and 1990s,

00:19:26.090 --> 00:19:29.123
addiction has become
more common among Lisu.

00:19:30.670 --> 00:19:33.870
The collapse of indigenous
Lisu cultural practices

00:19:33.870 --> 00:19:36.940
and the Christianization
and Chinese development

00:19:36.940 --> 00:19:39.600
has also disrupted the ritual context

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:43.043
that provides structure for
Lisu drinking activities.

00:19:45.540 --> 00:19:46.469
The non-Christian,

00:19:46.469 --> 00:19:50.160
alcohol drinking men's
attachment to alcohol

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:53.473
takes place within this
complex historical landscape.

00:19:55.070 --> 00:19:56.980
By the time I visited Goza,

00:19:56.980 --> 00:20:00.180
most elders in the village
identified as Christians.

00:20:00.180 --> 00:20:02.940
The alcohol drinking men,
whom I spent time with

00:20:02.940 --> 00:20:05.480
were the few elders in the village

00:20:05.480 --> 00:20:07.463
who remained non-Christians.

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:10.740
There were about a dozen of them,

00:20:10.740 --> 00:20:13.330
a handful or particularly close.

00:20:13.330 --> 00:20:16.410
Uncle Zoda and his best
friend, uncle Mobi,

00:20:16.410 --> 00:20:20.880
uncle Sapo, and his two brothers,
uncle Sathi and uncle Saki

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:23.363
and the younger uncle Yosa and uncle Aki.

00:20:24.550 --> 00:20:26.010
So following Lisu ethical

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:30.400
and using Lisu kinship terms
such as uncle (mumbles)

00:20:30.400 --> 00:20:32.300
to index intergenerational respect,

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:34.933
we got lots of formal kinship ties.

00:20:36.200 --> 00:20:38.510
They proudly proclaim themselves

00:20:38.510 --> 00:20:40.520
to be alcohol drinking men

00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:42.390
except for a couple of their wives.

00:20:42.390 --> 00:20:45.470
Most of the women in the
village were Christians.

00:20:45.470 --> 00:20:46.860
Although Christian villagers

00:20:46.860 --> 00:20:49.380
were generally on amicable terms

00:20:49.380 --> 00:20:51.220
with the non-Christian elders,

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:53.873
the tension between the
groups were obvious.

00:20:55.680 --> 00:20:58.740
During my fieldwork,
these men drank regularly

00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:02.560
some like uncle Zoda and uncle Mobi,

00:21:02.560 --> 00:21:05.700
drank as often and as much as they could,

00:21:05.700 --> 00:21:07.800
others drank frequently,

00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:09.470
but with more control.

00:21:09.470 --> 00:21:11.270
Uncle Sapo and his brothers,

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:14.177
uncle Sathi and uncle Saki, for example,

00:21:14.177 --> 00:21:16.360
who usually brings drink once

00:21:16.360 --> 00:21:19.130
or twice a month for several days,

00:21:19.130 --> 00:21:22.400
and then spend the rest of
the month relatively sober.

00:21:22.400 --> 00:21:24.450
This cyclical binge drinking resemble

00:21:24.450 --> 00:21:27.410
the old drinking pattern
the auntie Yona said

00:21:27.410 --> 00:21:29.740
was common in the earlier days

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:32.210
when men used to drink
for big celebrations

00:21:32.210 --> 00:21:33.923
or for important rituals.

00:21:35.182 --> 00:21:38.050
The habits of the men were partly shaped

00:21:38.050 --> 00:21:41.040
by their own personal
histories and circumstances,

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:43.900
for example, uncle Zoda's
compulsive drinking

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:47.340
resulted in part from the
chronic pain he suffered

00:21:47.340 --> 00:21:49.440
from having endured forced sterilization

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:52.843
under China's birth planning
policy in the 1990s.

00:21:53.810 --> 00:21:54.973
His sons frequent ventures

00:21:54.973 --> 00:21:57.740
to drink away from home with his friends

00:21:57.740 --> 00:21:59.830
was intern probably related

00:21:59.830 --> 00:22:02.800
to his father's rowdy
drinking gatherings at home

00:22:02.800 --> 00:22:06.093
which drove the son to find
other venues of release.

00:22:07.150 --> 00:22:10.418
In general, older drinkers
tended to enjoy liquor

00:22:10.418 --> 00:22:14.970
while younger drinkers, like
uncle Zoda's son, liked beer,

00:22:14.970 --> 00:22:17.560
but older young, all
these two alcohol drinkers

00:22:17.560 --> 00:22:19.370
I met in Goza and beyond

00:22:19.370 --> 00:22:21.593
preferred homemade liquor and beer.

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:25.500
During my stay in (mumbles),

00:22:26.390 --> 00:22:28.780
I lived with uncle Zoda and auntie Yona

00:22:28.780 --> 00:22:30.300
who had two children,

00:22:30.300 --> 00:22:31.693
a son and a daughter.

00:22:32.590 --> 00:22:35.165
Uncle Zoda and his son were not Christian.

00:22:35.165 --> 00:22:39.113
Auntie Yona on the other
hand was a devout Christian.

00:22:39.958 --> 00:22:42.920
Their daughter, Nana was
a Christian by default

00:22:42.920 --> 00:22:46.200
because of her gender and
because of her mother's faith.

00:22:46.200 --> 00:22:47.082
Nana was my classmate

00:22:47.082 --> 00:22:50.320
and my Lisu language
tutor in Minzu University

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:52.610
in the provincial capital of Kunming

00:22:52.610 --> 00:22:55.163
where I was enrolled as
a Lisu language student.

00:22:56.620 --> 00:22:58.810
Like many younger members of the village,

00:22:58.810 --> 00:23:01.263
Nana and her brother were usually away.

00:23:02.870 --> 00:23:04.610
Nana was attending university

00:23:04.610 --> 00:23:08.040
and her brother was working in
the factories in the cities.

00:23:08.040 --> 00:23:09.970
Their absence meant that...

00:23:15.400 --> 00:23:17.580
Until the fields planted maize,

00:23:17.580 --> 00:23:18.760
weeded the grounds,

00:23:18.760 --> 00:23:20.200
scavenged for pig feed

00:23:20.200 --> 00:23:23.150
and cut firewood alongside auntie Yona.

00:23:23.150 --> 00:23:25.780
Through these everyday work activities,

00:23:25.780 --> 00:23:27.690
auntie Yona taught me Lisu.

00:23:27.690 --> 00:23:29.190
As I gained (mumbles) in Lisu,

00:23:30.100 --> 00:23:31.830
I conducted most of my research

00:23:31.830 --> 00:23:33.363
in the language of my host.

00:23:38.100 --> 00:23:40.420
Although this paper focuses on Lisu men,

00:23:40.420 --> 00:23:43.320
I spent most of my days in
the village with Lisu women.

00:23:45.879 --> 00:23:49.210
The women are preparing the vegetables

00:23:49.210 --> 00:23:51.540
to sell in the marketplace the next day,

00:23:51.540 --> 00:23:53.840
and I think this was
already midnight by then.

00:23:55.040 --> 00:23:56.430
For many of the women,

00:23:56.430 --> 00:23:59.870
alcohol drinking husbands
were the heaviest burden.

00:23:59.870 --> 00:24:01.750
As the men partied through the night,

00:24:01.750 --> 00:24:05.870
the women dealt with
everyday mundane realities,

00:24:05.870 --> 00:24:07.880
like for example,

00:24:07.880 --> 00:24:09.260
getting the vegetables prepared

00:24:09.260 --> 00:24:12.483
at midnight to sell in the market there.

00:24:13.880 --> 00:24:16.290
I did not participate in drinking alcohol,

00:24:16.290 --> 00:24:18.647
but I was often included as an observer

00:24:18.647 --> 00:24:21.130
and a welcome photographer.

00:24:21.130 --> 00:24:23.100
During these rowdy gatherings,

00:24:23.100 --> 00:24:27.250
I was frankly, often tired
from working in the fields

00:24:27.250 --> 00:24:28.460
and I wish I could rest

00:24:28.460 --> 00:24:31.680
instead of attending
to the men's (mumbles)

00:24:31.680 --> 00:24:33.653
Auntie Yona now was exhausted as well.

00:24:34.570 --> 00:24:37.310
And I always felt there
was a shared understanding

00:24:37.310 --> 00:24:39.470
between auntie Yona and the men

00:24:39.470 --> 00:24:43.820
as if their drinking was
also a catharsis for her.

00:24:43.820 --> 00:24:45.430
Although she did not drink,

00:24:45.430 --> 00:24:49.040
she joined them as they joke
and make fun of each other.

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:50.317
Following her example

00:24:50.317 --> 00:24:54.320
and began to listen more
carefully to the drunken speech

00:24:54.320 --> 00:24:57.163
of the alcohol drinking
men in (mumbles) village.

00:24:59.250 --> 00:25:01.610
So now I'm moving on to the next section

00:25:01.610 --> 00:25:04.820
where I'm going to be talking
about melancholic play

00:25:04.820 --> 00:25:06.720
in the night of drinking with the men.

00:25:16.620 --> 00:25:18.670
One ordinary wintry evening,

00:25:18.670 --> 00:25:21.090
not long after the pigs were fed

00:25:21.090 --> 00:25:23.050
and the chicken feed prepared,

00:25:23.050 --> 00:25:25.283
a group of men came to visit uncle Zoda.

00:25:26.300 --> 00:25:28.560
Heavy footsteps on the bamboo porch

00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:31.210
alerted us to their arrival.

00:25:31.210 --> 00:25:33.410
It was the usual (mumbles)

00:25:33.410 --> 00:25:36.480
uncle Mobi, the man
condemned by an old curse,

00:25:36.480 --> 00:25:39.750
uncle Sapo, the retired
sorcerer in the village,

00:25:39.750 --> 00:25:43.710
uncle Api, the villager
afflicted with epilepsy,

00:25:43.710 --> 00:25:45.730
uncle Yosa, the divorcee

00:25:45.730 --> 00:25:47.500
who is captured by Burmese soldiers

00:25:47.500 --> 00:25:49.580
and presumed dead for seven years,

00:25:49.580 --> 00:25:52.623
and uncle Aki, the widower
whose wife went nerd.

00:25:53.530 --> 00:25:56.110
These were uncle Zoda's blood brothers,

00:25:56.110 --> 00:25:57.780
fellow non Christian men

00:25:57.780 --> 00:26:00.440
who found solidarity with each other

00:26:00.440 --> 00:26:03.793
in their shared love for
alcohol and the past.

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:06.690
Auntie Yona welcomed them.

00:26:06.690 --> 00:26:08.200
She served them food,

00:26:08.200 --> 00:26:09.480
which they declined,

00:26:09.480 --> 00:26:11.610
and shared uncle Zoda's alcohol with them

00:26:11.610 --> 00:26:13.373
which they eagerly accepted.

00:26:14.230 --> 00:26:17.913
The men had gathered to celebrate
the return of uncle Aki.

00:26:18.990 --> 00:26:21.340
Aki was the youngest of the lot

00:26:21.340 --> 00:26:22.490
and often left the village

00:26:22.490 --> 00:26:24.720
for long periods of
working construction jobs

00:26:24.720 --> 00:26:28.060
in the Northern part of the County.

00:26:28.060 --> 00:26:31.490
Actually, it was the Northern
County above the prefecture.

00:26:31.490 --> 00:26:33.380
Passing bottle after bottle

00:26:33.380 --> 00:26:36.550
and cup after cup of alcohol,

00:26:36.550 --> 00:26:38.630
the men chattered, laughed,

00:26:38.630 --> 00:26:40.807
sang and cried in joy.

00:26:40.807 --> 00:26:43.737
"Drink alcohol!" Uncle Zoda cried,

00:26:43.737 --> 00:26:45.657
"Let's talk and joke together,

00:26:45.657 --> 00:26:47.780
"but no fighting, no quarreling."

00:26:47.780 --> 00:26:50.343
Uncle Sapo, the eldest of them said.

00:26:52.220 --> 00:26:55.600
Alcohol circulated
continuously among them.

00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:58.340
The gathering began relatively calmly,

00:26:58.340 --> 00:27:02.520
but rose quickly to a
crescendo of inebriated chaos,

00:27:02.520 --> 00:27:06.221
loud laughter, howls,
reverberated through the room.

00:27:06.221 --> 00:27:09.760
Lisu pop music videos blasted
from Zoda's television

00:27:09.760 --> 00:27:11.830
providing the shrill accompaniment

00:27:11.830 --> 00:27:14.310
to this carnival esque scene.

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:15.870
The incandescent bulb

00:27:15.870 --> 00:27:18.160
that hung above the flashing TV

00:27:18.160 --> 00:27:20.680
and the burning flame from the campfire

00:27:20.680 --> 00:27:23.030
pass moving shadows around the room,

00:27:23.030 --> 00:27:24.780
giving the strange effect

00:27:24.780 --> 00:27:28.513
that the house was even
darker than it already was.

00:27:30.230 --> 00:27:32.040
Excited by my camera,

00:27:32.040 --> 00:27:34.360
the men insisted I take pictures of them,

00:27:34.360 --> 00:27:36.470
drunk and half naked.

00:27:36.470 --> 00:27:39.080
Zoda sharing beer with Mobi in a bowl,

00:27:39.080 --> 00:27:42.220
Aki pretending to be a
corpse hanging from a tree,

00:27:42.220 --> 00:27:44.973
and me pretending to share
a cigarette with Aki.

00:27:45.900 --> 00:27:49.300
Each of these acts was
a reference to the past.

00:27:49.300 --> 00:27:52.330
Zoda and Mobi sharing a
beer from the same bowl

00:27:52.330 --> 00:27:53.803
resemble the cross cupping practice

00:27:53.803 --> 00:27:55.015
that Lisu used to perform

00:27:55.015 --> 00:27:57.860
during weddings and other celebrations.

00:27:57.860 --> 00:28:00.030
Uncle Aki pretending to be a corpse

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:01.890
reminded me of the Lisu story

00:28:01.890 --> 00:28:03.900
about the star crossed lovers

00:28:03.900 --> 00:28:06.380
who hang themselves from a tree.

00:28:06.380 --> 00:28:08.090
The cigarettes were new,

00:28:08.090 --> 00:28:10.560
but they represented the traditional pipes

00:28:10.560 --> 00:28:12.053
that Lisu used to smoke.

00:28:12.960 --> 00:28:14.380
In their drunkenness,

00:28:14.380 --> 00:28:18.110
the men broadcast their
skewed imaginings of the past,

00:28:18.110 --> 00:28:22.010
the liquor mix of half
memories, false memories

00:28:22.010 --> 00:28:24.280
and incomplete, erroneous stories

00:28:24.280 --> 00:28:26.965
about things, people and events

00:28:26.965 --> 00:28:29.633
that may never have actually been.

00:28:30.537 --> 00:28:32.920
"I am a prophet!" Uncle Zoda called out

00:28:32.920 --> 00:28:35.527
provoking much laughter among the men.

00:28:35.527 --> 00:28:37.100
"Bring me meat!"

00:28:37.100 --> 00:28:40.040
Many of the sorceress who
converted to Christianity

00:28:40.040 --> 00:28:43.060
became Christian prophets. (mumbles)

00:28:43.060 --> 00:28:46.740
Uncle enjoyed making fun
of these sorcerer prophets.

00:28:46.740 --> 00:28:49.410
He saw them as sorcerers
trying to cheat their way

00:28:49.410 --> 00:28:51.997
into the Christians hearts and pockets.

00:28:51.997 --> 00:28:53.447
"Their true aim," He said,

00:28:53.447 --> 00:28:56.247
"Was to earn as much meat as possible."

00:28:57.227 --> 00:29:00.673
"Tell me the next lottery
number uncle." I joked with him.

00:29:01.761 --> 00:29:03.230
Zoda took a pause

00:29:03.230 --> 00:29:06.437
as if trying to find the
number with his mind's eye.

00:29:06.437 --> 00:29:09.817
"I don't see them,"
(mumbles) Zoda replied,

00:29:09.817 --> 00:29:12.877
"Come back tomorrow, if the
laughter reverberates it."

00:29:14.076 --> 00:29:17.880
The boundaries between the
living and the undead blurred,

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:21.690
the more the men drank, the
closer the spirits came to them.

00:29:21.690 --> 00:29:25.630
As the men descended into
alcohol madness, (mumbles)

00:29:25.630 --> 00:29:30.030
Their (mumbles) speech
indicated the possible presence

00:29:30.030 --> 00:29:32.853
of something other than human.

00:29:34.907 --> 00:29:37.327
"Take a picture. Take a picture.

00:29:37.327 --> 00:29:39.580
"I am a demon here to kill humans.

00:29:39.580 --> 00:29:41.879
(speaking Lisu language)

00:29:41.879 --> 00:29:44.034
"I am a demon here to eat humans."

00:29:44.034 --> 00:29:46.100
(speaking Lisu language)

00:29:46.100 --> 00:29:48.600
Shouted an alcohol mad, uncle Mobi.

00:29:48.600 --> 00:29:52.233
As he insisted that I take
a picture of him and Zoda.

00:29:53.620 --> 00:29:56.540
Pretending he was holding a Lisu machete,

00:29:56.540 --> 00:29:58.410
Mobi stood behind Zoda,

00:29:58.410 --> 00:30:01.933
acting like a demon about
to kill an oblivious Zoda.

00:30:03.230 --> 00:30:04.880
The alcohol mad Mobi,

00:30:04.880 --> 00:30:08.180
ready himself to conduct
an imaginary murder.

00:30:08.180 --> 00:30:10.410
Possessed by the spirit,

00:30:10.410 --> 00:30:13.810
Mobi was no longer fully present.

00:30:13.810 --> 00:30:16.310
As was common on such occasions,

00:30:16.310 --> 00:30:21.120
the absent Mobi went to a
lasting trace of his demon self.

00:30:21.120 --> 00:30:22.500
My Nikon camera

00:30:22.500 --> 00:30:25.980
with its ability to capture
his absent presence,

00:30:25.980 --> 00:30:28.223
became an object of desire.

00:30:30.740 --> 00:30:34.651
In Zoda's house spirit
and men run (mumbles)

00:30:34.651 --> 00:30:37.860
Like the stigmatized
men who summoned them,

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:41.025
the spirits were beings
denied recognition.

00:30:41.025 --> 00:30:44.320
Formerly powerful and respected spirits,

00:30:44.320 --> 00:30:46.440
they had turned into downtrodden,

00:30:46.440 --> 00:30:49.360
forgotten and dismissed demons,

00:30:49.360 --> 00:30:53.460
wronged by the circumstances
of their life and death,

00:30:53.460 --> 00:30:56.230
wronged by the Christians
who betrayed them,

00:30:56.230 --> 00:30:57.810
and wronged by the Chinese state

00:30:57.810 --> 00:31:00.890
that branded them as
primitive superstitions.

00:31:00.890 --> 00:31:03.850
The demonized spirits were unable to rest.

00:31:03.850 --> 00:31:06.280
Identifying with these demons,

00:31:06.280 --> 00:31:09.570
the men let the spirits
speak, allowing them

00:31:09.570 --> 00:31:13.710
to express the shared
grievances of men and spirit.

00:31:13.710 --> 00:31:15.003
In their drunkenness,

00:31:15.003 --> 00:31:17.380
the men took on the dual role

00:31:17.380 --> 00:31:19.610
of the sorceress seeking justice

00:31:19.610 --> 00:31:22.640
and the demon wronged by others.

00:31:22.640 --> 00:31:25.860
Rather than trying to free
themselves from the spirits,

00:31:25.860 --> 00:31:28.300
they summoned them to the present.

00:31:28.300 --> 00:31:29.960
Their melancholy howls

00:31:29.960 --> 00:31:32.440
pierced the silence of the village night,

00:31:32.440 --> 00:31:34.910
jolting their Christian neighbors awake,

00:31:34.910 --> 00:31:37.600
making them listen to the sounds

00:31:37.600 --> 00:31:40.483
and sorrows of banish ancestors.

00:31:43.680 --> 00:31:45.300
As the night went on,

00:31:45.300 --> 00:31:49.730
the men descended deeper and
deeper into alcohol madness,

00:31:49.730 --> 00:31:51.950
hurling insults at each other.

00:31:51.950 --> 00:31:54.260
They pruned out of tune music

00:31:54.260 --> 00:31:56.910
and gave in decipherable speeches

00:31:56.910 --> 00:31:59.890
about themselves and their pasts.

00:31:59.890 --> 00:32:01.003
Among the men,

00:32:01.840 --> 00:32:04.953
Mobi and Zoda were the
most resilient drinkers.

00:32:05.850 --> 00:32:08.490
As everyone began to wonder home,

00:32:08.490 --> 00:32:10.440
Mobi stayed with Zoda.

00:32:10.440 --> 00:32:15.370
The two ate each other on to
drink more and play harder.

00:32:15.370 --> 00:32:16.462
The next morning,

00:32:16.462 --> 00:32:19.460
sleep deprived aunt Yona and I

00:32:19.460 --> 00:32:21.530
found them both unconscious.

00:32:21.530 --> 00:32:24.320
Mobi laid next to the dead
campfire on the floor,

00:32:24.320 --> 00:32:28.103
Zoda was rolled on his bed, motionless.

00:32:29.680 --> 00:32:32.490
In the dead of the
night, when others sleep,

00:32:32.490 --> 00:32:36.180
the men called upon the
spirits with their alcohol.

00:32:36.180 --> 00:32:39.790
Together, men and spirit danced, sang

00:32:39.790 --> 00:32:42.660
and haunted the quiet orderly lives

00:32:42.660 --> 00:32:44.960
of their Christian neighbors.

00:32:44.960 --> 00:32:47.810
Drinking alcohol, the men's bodies

00:32:47.810 --> 00:32:50.130
became the medium through which spirits

00:32:50.130 --> 00:32:53.100
re-entered the world of the living.

00:32:53.100 --> 00:32:55.260
Dancing and singing together,

00:32:55.260 --> 00:32:59.230
spirit and men honor
their ties to each other.

00:32:59.230 --> 00:33:02.140
The despair of loss was transfigured

00:33:02.140 --> 00:33:04.290
into the joys of existence,

00:33:04.290 --> 00:33:07.170
forgotten memories into living rituals

00:33:07.170 --> 00:33:09.450
demons into ancestors,

00:33:09.450 --> 00:33:12.800
alcohol drinking men
into powerful sorcerers

00:33:12.800 --> 00:33:14.883
and death back into life.

00:33:21.630 --> 00:33:24.504
Drinking is an act of togetherness.

00:33:24.504 --> 00:33:25.970
Alcohol brought together

00:33:25.970 --> 00:33:28.370
the alcohol drinking men in (mumbles)

00:33:28.370 --> 00:33:30.580
as brothers with a shared commitment

00:33:30.580 --> 00:33:34.873
to remembering lost practices
and forgotten ancestors.

00:33:36.350 --> 00:33:38.050
Whereas the Christians in the village

00:33:38.050 --> 00:33:41.080
sought to break away from old practices,

00:33:41.080 --> 00:33:44.810
the alcohol drinking
men refused to move on.

00:33:44.810 --> 00:33:47.970
Their melancholia united
them as a community

00:33:47.970 --> 00:33:50.923
against the Christian
majority in the village.

00:33:52.827 --> 00:33:55.247
"We are the alcohol drinking men.

00:33:55.247 --> 00:33:57.137
"We work when we feel like working,

00:33:57.137 --> 00:33:59.307
"stop when we feel like stopping,

00:33:59.307 --> 00:34:01.717
"not like those people who go to church,

00:34:01.717 --> 00:34:04.180
"they are full of superstitions."

00:34:04.180 --> 00:34:07.000
Uncle Saki, proudly proclaimed once,

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:09.460
turning a typical Christian insult back

00:34:09.460 --> 00:34:11.053
at the Christians themselves.

00:34:12.240 --> 00:34:14.160
The men purposefully went

00:34:14.160 --> 00:34:17.530
against those things that
Christian said and did.

00:34:17.530 --> 00:34:19.060
Christians attended church

00:34:19.060 --> 00:34:21.110
and did not work on Sundays.

00:34:21.110 --> 00:34:23.540
Non-Christians had alcohol
drinking gatherings

00:34:23.540 --> 00:34:27.210
and worked any day of
the week or not work.

00:34:27.210 --> 00:34:29.190
Christians did not eat the blood

00:34:29.190 --> 00:34:31.550
and internal organs of animals,

00:34:31.550 --> 00:34:33.800
alcohol drinking men on the other hand,

00:34:33.800 --> 00:34:36.330
praised them as delicacies.

00:34:36.330 --> 00:34:40.487
Christians considered phrases
such as "Thank you," (mumbles)

00:34:40.487 --> 00:34:42.050
"Forgive me," (mumbles)

00:34:42.050 --> 00:34:44.780
and "How are you," (mumbles) polite.

00:34:44.780 --> 00:34:48.380
Non-Christians found
these expressions phony.

00:34:48.380 --> 00:34:51.108
The alcohol drinking uncles (mumbles)

00:34:51.108 --> 00:34:53.513
enjoyed poking fun at the Christians.

00:34:54.557 --> 00:34:56.567
"The best time to steal meat

00:34:56.567 --> 00:34:59.777
"is when Christians are
saying grace before a meal,

00:34:59.777 --> 00:35:01.477
"when they have their eyes shut

00:35:01.477 --> 00:35:04.127
"pick your favorite pieces
of meat from the dishes.

00:35:04.127 --> 00:35:05.587
"They won't even notice.

00:35:05.587 --> 00:35:08.167
"And if they do, you
can just call them out

00:35:08.167 --> 00:35:09.950
"for not praying properly."

00:35:09.950 --> 00:35:13.443
Uncle Mobi, uncle Zoda's
best friends taught me once.

00:35:14.460 --> 00:35:16.330
The men's witticism expanded

00:35:16.330 --> 00:35:19.120
beyond making fun of
their Christian neighbors.

00:35:19.120 --> 00:35:21.761
They made jokes about
cunning Han Chinese migrants;

00:35:21.761 --> 00:35:23.810
strange foreigners,

00:35:23.810 --> 00:35:27.230
book smart, but otherwise
stupid university students,

00:35:27.230 --> 00:35:28.890
bewitching Burmese women,

00:35:28.890 --> 00:35:30.640
lying Christian missionaries

00:35:30.640 --> 00:35:33.143
and greedy Chinese communist official.

00:35:35.200 --> 00:35:37.800
Once the government
distributed each household

00:35:37.800 --> 00:35:40.090
some walnut cuttings to plant.

00:35:40.090 --> 00:35:42.260
Although walnuts are lucrative,

00:35:42.260 --> 00:35:44.217
many Lisu avoid eating them.

00:35:44.217 --> 00:35:46.637
"Eating too much Walnut oil," they say,

00:35:46.637 --> 00:35:49.107
"Can turn a person into an idiot."

00:35:50.157 --> 00:35:53.157
"Si Ching Ping must like
eating a lot of walnuts,

00:35:53.157 --> 00:35:55.827
"that's why he keeps
asking us to plant walnuts.

00:35:55.827 --> 00:35:57.400
"No wonder he's so stupid."

00:35:57.400 --> 00:36:00.603
Uncle Zoda said as we
planted walnut cuttings.

00:36:02.930 --> 00:36:04.900
As the men pass alcohol around,

00:36:04.900 --> 00:36:08.550
they reversed Chinese and
Christian discourses of gratitude.

00:36:08.550 --> 00:36:11.570
There were no longer the ungrateful ones

00:36:11.570 --> 00:36:15.730
for rejecting the gifts of
Christianity and development.

00:36:15.730 --> 00:36:19.620
It was instead the Christians
who were ungrateful

00:36:19.620 --> 00:36:21.820
having forgotten about the spirits,

00:36:21.820 --> 00:36:24.620
having abandoned their
brothers and sisters,

00:36:24.620 --> 00:36:28.860
and having ultimately given
up what it means to be Lisu.

00:36:30.637 --> 00:36:35.070
Breathing new life, to
old reciprocal ties,

00:36:35.070 --> 00:36:38.380
the men rebuilt a community from ruins,

00:36:38.380 --> 00:36:40.380
from the ruins of loss

00:36:40.380 --> 00:36:42.330
and reminded that fellow villagers,

00:36:42.330 --> 00:36:44.580
Christians and non-Christians alike

00:36:44.580 --> 00:36:46.104
that they were not yet dead.

00:36:46.104 --> 00:36:49.107
As uncle Mobi used to defiantly yell,

00:36:49.107 --> 00:36:51.860
"I cannot die." (mumbles)

00:36:51.860 --> 00:36:53.370
Refusing to let go,

00:36:53.370 --> 00:36:56.500
the men held on together to fragments

00:36:56.500 --> 00:36:59.110
of what it means to be Lisu

00:36:59.110 --> 00:37:03.043
outside of Christian and
Chinese civilizational frames.

00:37:05.200 --> 00:37:06.943
So I'm now going to conclude.

00:37:17.000 --> 00:37:19.100
Around two years after I left (mumbles)

00:37:19.100 --> 00:37:21.160
I received a message from Nana

00:37:21.160 --> 00:37:23.670
letting me know that
her father, uncle Zoda,

00:37:23.670 --> 00:37:25.540
was critically ill.

00:37:25.540 --> 00:37:28.710
His organs were failing and he was dying.

00:37:28.710 --> 00:37:31.030
Upon receiving the news
of uncle's illness,

00:37:31.030 --> 00:37:33.450
I prepared my return to China,

00:37:33.450 --> 00:37:35.220
but as I was getting ready to go back,

00:37:35.220 --> 00:37:37.630
COVID-19 hit Wuhan

00:37:37.630 --> 00:37:40.689
and travels to China
soon became impossible.

00:37:40.689 --> 00:37:42.880
(mumbles) village went under lockdown.

00:37:42.880 --> 00:37:44.610
Villagers were instructed to remain

00:37:44.610 --> 00:37:47.060
in their own homes and villages.

00:37:47.060 --> 00:37:49.290
I called uncle Zoda one afternoon

00:37:49.290 --> 00:37:51.853
aware that it might be
our last conversation.

00:37:52.757 --> 00:37:55.817
"I'm still here," He
said almost cheerfully,

00:37:55.817 --> 00:37:57.287
"Not dead yet.

00:37:57.287 --> 00:37:59.587
"These days we cannot go anywhere.

00:37:59.587 --> 00:38:01.927
"Even the demons cannot move.

00:38:01.927 --> 00:38:05.147
"If demons don't move,
people can't move on either.

00:38:05.147 --> 00:38:07.717
"I'm just hanging out, watching TV,

00:38:07.717 --> 00:38:10.477
"sitting by the fire next to the demons,

00:38:10.477 --> 00:38:11.930
"not dead yet."

00:38:11.930 --> 00:38:13.860
He burst into laughter.

00:38:13.860 --> 00:38:15.470
I laughed with him.

00:38:15.470 --> 00:38:19.090
Zoda continued to play
in the face of death.

00:38:19.090 --> 00:38:22.480
Melancholia once more transformed

00:38:22.480 --> 00:38:24.693
into a half funny joke.

00:38:28.170 --> 00:38:31.020
This talk has examined how low status

00:38:31.020 --> 00:38:33.430
Lisu alcohol drinking men,

00:38:33.430 --> 00:38:37.870
play with melancholy as an act of refusal.

00:38:37.870 --> 00:38:39.430
Within communities struggling

00:38:39.430 --> 00:38:42.320
for their cultural and spiritual survival,

00:38:42.320 --> 00:38:46.763
like playing with melancholia
can be a mode of refusal.

00:38:48.010 --> 00:38:49.208
As I have argued,

00:38:49.208 --> 00:38:53.050
the alcohol drinking men's
melancholia is a refusal

00:38:53.050 --> 00:38:55.018
because it is constituted

00:38:55.018 --> 00:38:59.010
by a playful and willingness to let go

00:38:59.010 --> 00:39:01.793
rather than an inability to overcome.

00:39:03.520 --> 00:39:05.170
By the same token,

00:39:05.170 --> 00:39:07.370
their refusal is melancholic

00:39:07.370 --> 00:39:11.480
because it is characterized
by an attachment to things

00:39:11.480 --> 00:39:13.623
that are supposed to be dead.

00:39:14.990 --> 00:39:19.840
Infusing grief with play, humor and joy,

00:39:19.840 --> 00:39:22.830
the men draw on despair, loss,

00:39:22.830 --> 00:39:25.931
and suffering as a cultural resource

00:39:25.931 --> 00:39:30.660
for the reconstitution of
community within, but apart

00:39:30.660 --> 00:39:32.130
from the dominant Christian

00:39:32.130 --> 00:39:34.563
and Chinese frames of civilization.

00:39:36.070 --> 00:39:38.920
Ambivalently mixing sadness and joy,

00:39:38.920 --> 00:39:43.130
tragedy and comedy,
forgetting and remembering,

00:39:43.130 --> 00:39:45.890
the melancholic play illustrates

00:39:45.890 --> 00:39:50.840
the complex contradictions
constituting experiences of grief

00:39:50.840 --> 00:39:54.043
that shape intimate everyday politics.

00:40:01.550 --> 00:40:03.548
Refusal through melancholic play,

00:40:03.548 --> 00:40:06.220
speaks to cross-cultural experiences

00:40:06.220 --> 00:40:09.260
of how people recuperate and regenerate

00:40:09.260 --> 00:40:11.883
in the thrills of colonial domination.

00:40:13.130 --> 00:40:15.420
Indigenous and minority communities

00:40:15.420 --> 00:40:18.790
are often portrayed as damaged victims,

00:40:18.790 --> 00:40:21.760
but there is no doubt that
these communities struggle

00:40:21.760 --> 00:40:24.360
with great injustice and oppression.

00:40:24.360 --> 00:40:28.570
People face suffering, not
only with anger and despair,

00:40:28.570 --> 00:40:31.150
but also with humor and play.

00:40:31.150 --> 00:40:32.850
Illness and afflictions

00:40:32.850 --> 00:40:34.860
such as alcohol dependency

00:40:34.860 --> 00:40:36.860
are symptoms of victim hood,

00:40:36.860 --> 00:40:40.800
but they are also acts
through which people convey,

00:40:40.800 --> 00:40:43.830
communicate and share their experiences

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:48.200
by attending to jokes and social histories

00:40:48.200 --> 00:40:50.069
of men who might be considered

00:40:50.069 --> 00:40:53.620
by public health experts as alcoholic.

00:40:53.620 --> 00:40:56.760
I pay attention to the social commentaries

00:40:56.760 --> 00:40:59.830
and historical memories they articulate

00:40:59.830 --> 00:41:02.010
through their affliction.

00:41:02.010 --> 00:41:05.490
Situating such afflictions
as alcohol dependency

00:41:05.490 --> 00:41:08.120
in relation to these
historical structures,

00:41:08.120 --> 00:41:10.673
reverses the disease discourse.

00:41:11.530 --> 00:41:15.230
The pathology lies not in the sick person,

00:41:15.230 --> 00:41:17.560
but in the social and political structures

00:41:17.560 --> 00:41:19.363
within the illness emerge.

00:41:23.700 --> 00:41:25.733
Within which the illness emerge.

00:41:27.008 --> 00:41:30.170
Lisu alcohol drinking
men's melancholic refusal

00:41:30.170 --> 00:41:32.110
provides inspiration for thinking

00:41:32.110 --> 00:41:34.460
about how to combine dark anthropology

00:41:34.460 --> 00:41:37.230
and the anthropology of the good.

00:41:37.230 --> 00:41:39.610
Proponents of the anthropology of the good

00:41:39.610 --> 00:41:42.470
critique contemporarily
anthropology's tendency

00:41:42.470 --> 00:41:44.960
to universalize the suffering subject

00:41:44.960 --> 00:41:48.120
which they argue is a
modern day replacement

00:41:48.120 --> 00:41:50.520
of the savage slot.

00:41:50.520 --> 00:41:53.160
Similarly, indigenous
scholars have proposed

00:41:53.160 --> 00:41:55.600
a moratorium on damaged centered research

00:41:55.600 --> 00:41:57.547
that portrays indigenous communities

00:41:57.547 --> 00:42:01.040
as only, or mainly damaged.

00:42:01.040 --> 00:42:04.020
But this call to suspend
damage center research

00:42:04.020 --> 00:42:06.160
is not about denying damage

00:42:06.160 --> 00:42:10.780
nor about turning our
attention towards only the good

00:42:10.780 --> 00:42:14.820
rather it is a call for
an epistemological shift

00:42:14.820 --> 00:42:18.000
in the way research is
conducted and motivated.

00:42:18.000 --> 00:42:20.720
Activist anthropology
in which anthropologists

00:42:20.720 --> 00:42:24.140
are directly involved in the
social movements they study,

00:42:24.140 --> 00:42:26.340
provides an important starting point

00:42:26.340 --> 00:42:30.420
for rethinking the theories of
change undergirding research.

00:42:30.420 --> 00:42:32.590
Part of this epistemological shift

00:42:32.590 --> 00:42:35.710
towards research for
change and transformation

00:42:35.710 --> 00:42:38.450
is to recognize people as complex subjects

00:42:38.450 --> 00:42:40.940
who shift in contradictory desires.

00:42:40.940 --> 00:42:45.193
Desires that simultaneously
resist and reproduce power.

00:42:46.510 --> 00:42:50.180
Lisu men's melancholic alcohol drinking

00:42:50.180 --> 00:42:53.180
is constituted by such complex interplays

00:42:53.180 --> 00:42:55.510
between despair and joy

00:42:55.510 --> 00:42:58.210
and between loss and regeneration.

00:42:58.210 --> 00:43:02.110
Their melancholia is not just
an embodiment of victim hood,

00:43:02.110 --> 00:43:06.200
but also the grounds on
which they refuse colonialism

00:43:06.200 --> 00:43:08.033
and celebrate their survival.

00:43:08.900 --> 00:43:10.410
Theories of the subject

00:43:10.410 --> 00:43:14.414
that fully recognize such
complex and contradictory desires

00:43:14.414 --> 00:43:18.670
(mumbles) a critical foundation
for building solidarities

00:43:18.670 --> 00:43:21.090
and for rethinking the theories of change

00:43:21.090 --> 00:43:23.293
undergirding anthropology research.

00:43:24.980 --> 00:43:25.813
Thank you.

00:43:32.260 --> 00:43:35.200
Thanks, very excited about your questions.

00:43:35.200 --> 00:43:37.613
- Thank you Ting for a fantastic talk.

00:43:38.753 --> 00:43:42.230
So we have plenty of time
for question and answer,

00:43:42.230 --> 00:43:44.160
but I'm mindful of people's time.

00:43:44.160 --> 00:43:46.460
I know some people have
to leave at one sometimes

00:43:46.460 --> 00:43:48.690
because of various time commitments.

00:43:48.690 --> 00:43:50.400
So I'd like to ask anyone who thinks

00:43:50.400 --> 00:43:51.810
they need to leave by one,

00:43:51.810 --> 00:43:53.733
please ask your question first.

00:43:54.660 --> 00:43:56.450
Otherwise, we'll just go in the order

00:43:56.450 --> 00:43:57.400
in which I see hands.

00:43:57.400 --> 00:44:00.300
So if you wanna say then
you need to ask first,

00:44:00.300 --> 00:44:01.560
put it in the chat.

00:44:01.560 --> 00:44:03.423
Otherwise we'll just go in an order.

00:44:05.410 --> 00:44:08.410
Is there anyone who has a time commitment?

00:44:08.410 --> 00:44:11.490
Otherwise I'll just call on El

00:44:11.490 --> 00:44:13.683
'cause I see El has a question.

00:44:14.920 --> 00:44:16.490
So El go ahead and ask your question

00:44:16.490 --> 00:44:17.560
and then if anyone else needs to,

00:44:17.560 --> 00:44:18.810
will follow up with that.

00:44:21.430 --> 00:44:22.807
- Nice to meet you. I'm a PhD student here

00:44:22.807 --> 00:44:24.660
in Anthropology and Environmental Studies.

00:44:24.660 --> 00:44:29.010
I used to be at Yale NUS.
So this is great. (laughs)

00:44:29.010 --> 00:44:31.310
So this is fascinating
and if this is a chapter,

00:44:31.310 --> 00:44:32.440
I'm looking forward the book.

00:44:32.440 --> 00:44:34.530
And so I'm still really excited.

00:44:34.530 --> 00:44:36.010
And I particularly was thinking

00:44:36.010 --> 00:44:37.820
about the ways that this is similar

00:44:37.820 --> 00:44:40.010
or different to like
parts of Northern Thailand

00:44:40.010 --> 00:44:42.670
where you have the Karen,
(mumbles) and the Burma as well

00:44:42.670 --> 00:44:44.170
and how the Christian Buddhist narrative

00:44:44.170 --> 00:44:45.979
is actually fundamentally very different.

00:44:45.979 --> 00:44:46.888
So that was kind of interesting

00:44:46.888 --> 00:44:48.289
for me to think through and compare it

00:44:48.289 --> 00:44:51.540
because to the Christians in the village,

00:44:51.540 --> 00:44:54.327
in Northern Ireland they were like, "Oh,

00:44:54.327 --> 00:44:55.567
"we're super open to Christians.

00:44:55.567 --> 00:44:56.947
"The Buddhists were
like the dominant people

00:44:56.947 --> 00:44:58.140
"and it's all great."

00:44:58.140 --> 00:44:58.973
So it was really interesting

00:44:58.973 --> 00:45:01.262
that very clear reversal there

00:45:01.262 --> 00:45:03.140
in terms of what I'm familiar with.

00:45:03.140 --> 00:45:05.671
But sorry my question was about ontology

00:45:05.671 --> 00:45:08.830
and I'm wondering how you
theorize ontology in a sense.

00:45:08.830 --> 00:45:10.710
So in one primary sense

00:45:10.710 --> 00:45:12.177
there's that (mumbles) and uncanny,

00:45:12.177 --> 00:45:13.370
and then what's interesting here

00:45:13.370 --> 00:45:15.440
is I feel like the
melancholy kind of comes

00:45:15.440 --> 00:45:18.120
into that kind of lack of re inability

00:45:18.120 --> 00:45:19.660
to kind of do away with repression,

00:45:19.660 --> 00:45:21.320
so that coming back with the repression.

00:45:21.320 --> 00:45:22.591
And it's really interesting

00:45:22.591 --> 00:45:25.400
and also to think about
the role of the spirits

00:45:25.400 --> 00:45:27.340
in terms of them being wrong,

00:45:27.340 --> 00:45:28.910
but then it kind of Andrew Johnson's book

00:45:28.910 --> 00:45:30.320
talked a lot more about ambivalent.

00:45:30.320 --> 00:45:31.340
So here the spirit seems

00:45:31.340 --> 00:45:33.520
to be a lot more active and and angry

00:45:33.520 --> 00:45:35.080
as opposed to like more ambivalent

00:45:35.080 --> 00:45:38.237
kind of relationship on the river.

00:45:38.237 --> 00:45:40.840
Another sense of oncology

00:45:40.840 --> 00:45:43.770
is this kind of social control.

00:45:43.770 --> 00:45:45.010
And so this idea

00:45:45.010 --> 00:45:46.953
about nations being built
on the zonal mass death

00:45:46.953 --> 00:45:49.310
is something like (mumbles)
talks about in China.

00:45:49.310 --> 00:45:51.070
And I'm wondering to what extent

00:45:51.070 --> 00:45:52.800
you can think about ontology

00:45:52.800 --> 00:45:54.866
as a molding kind of individual

00:45:54.866 --> 00:45:58.160
and where you're talking
about intra-Lisu identity

00:45:58.160 --> 00:46:00.420
but also kind of different
scales of identity

00:46:00.420 --> 00:46:02.110
in the kind of social control there.

00:46:02.110 --> 00:46:03.100
So I'm just wondering kind of like

00:46:03.100 --> 00:46:04.700
how you're thinking about...

00:46:04.700 --> 00:46:06.149
There's so much to do with the ethnography

00:46:06.149 --> 00:46:07.530
that's coming through.

00:46:07.530 --> 00:46:08.363
I'm just wondering like

00:46:08.363 --> 00:46:12.093
how you're putting all
those thoughts together?

00:46:18.650 --> 00:46:19.600
- [Ting] Thank you.

00:46:20.850 --> 00:46:21.923
I'll just respond.

00:46:25.452 --> 00:46:27.295
That's such a great question

00:46:27.295 --> 00:46:30.963
and I don't know if I can answer
completely, do it justice.

00:46:35.866 --> 00:46:39.150
So I think the way I'm
thinking about haunting

00:46:39.150 --> 00:46:43.620
is partly that indeed there
is this desire to move away.

00:46:43.620 --> 00:46:46.750
That we have overcome our indigenous

00:46:46.750 --> 00:46:49.357
or our kind of either
it is the simple past

00:46:49.357 --> 00:46:51.308
with a Christian perspective

00:46:51.308 --> 00:46:54.375
or it is that we're moving on

00:46:54.375 --> 00:46:57.340
from the backward at Lisu practices,

00:46:57.340 --> 00:47:00.250
and we're trying to become modern

00:47:00.250 --> 00:47:04.390
but that is never a complete
conversion in a sense.

00:47:04.390 --> 00:47:06.750
So the uncanny, it is
like an uncanny reminder

00:47:06.750 --> 00:47:08.970
that these men are living in this space.

00:47:08.970 --> 00:47:10.850
They're staying in this space

00:47:10.850 --> 00:47:15.040
that reminds the villagers
and the people around them

00:47:15.040 --> 00:47:17.400
that have never really actually,

00:47:17.400 --> 00:47:20.990
you're not really ever completely modern

00:47:20.990 --> 00:47:22.880
and actually

00:47:26.051 --> 00:47:27.950
the demons reside within you.

00:47:27.950 --> 00:47:32.273
And so there's perhaps the
haunting emerges in that sense.

00:47:35.550 --> 00:47:37.040
In the scale of...

00:47:37.040 --> 00:47:38.280
I thought that was so interesting,

00:47:38.280 --> 00:47:39.840
the nation built on mass death

00:47:39.840 --> 00:47:42.230
because I think there's this a place

00:47:42.230 --> 00:47:43.840
that is built on mass death.

00:47:43.840 --> 00:47:45.140
And so there is that scale,

00:47:45.140 --> 00:47:47.470
that's really helpful
for me to think through

00:47:47.470 --> 00:47:50.900
and include in my next
iteration of this chapter

00:47:50.900 --> 00:47:54.969
because there is that scale
of the village haunting,

00:47:54.969 --> 00:47:56.830
the men haunting the villagers

00:47:56.830 --> 00:47:58.720
by reminding them of the past.

00:47:58.720 --> 00:48:01.683
But there's also the scale
in which this whole place

00:48:01.683 --> 00:48:06.683
which is being promoted as a
tourist hotspot, as developed

00:48:06.770 --> 00:48:10.270
because Chinese state is
developing this place.

00:48:10.270 --> 00:48:14.030
It's modern. It's becoming
modern, there's no more poverty,

00:48:14.030 --> 00:48:16.470
but all of that narrative

00:48:16.470 --> 00:48:18.750
is built on so much death in the region.

00:48:18.750 --> 00:48:23.360
Cultural death of the
men that I've described,

00:48:23.360 --> 00:48:25.890
but also the physical death of the people

00:48:25.890 --> 00:48:27.490
who are dying in traffic accidents,

00:48:27.490 --> 00:48:29.100
who are dying in construction work,

00:48:29.100 --> 00:48:30.619
who are dying in landslides,

00:48:30.619 --> 00:48:33.880
who are dying because of
family problems and suicide

00:48:33.880 --> 00:48:36.150
and mental health and
alcoholism and sickness,

00:48:36.150 --> 00:48:38.190
and cancer and other kinds of new diseases

00:48:38.190 --> 00:48:40.110
that were never there in the first place,

00:48:40.110 --> 00:48:42.573
that have recently become for some reason

00:48:42.573 --> 00:48:46.390
like other parts of the world,
development is correlated

00:48:46.390 --> 00:48:49.420
with the rise of all these
noncommunicable diseases.

00:48:49.420 --> 00:48:50.750
And so there is that level,

00:48:50.750 --> 00:48:53.020
the level of that scale
that you're talking about

00:48:53.020 --> 00:48:56.360
where development is
actually built on mass death.

00:48:56.360 --> 00:48:57.763
And in a sense,

00:48:58.730 --> 00:49:01.980
this place is haunted by that death

00:49:01.980 --> 00:49:03.359
that is also being repressed

00:49:03.359 --> 00:49:06.738
by the discourse of development.

00:49:06.738 --> 00:49:08.038
And that's really helpful.

00:49:08.920 --> 00:49:10.603
And I think there's a
third level of haunting

00:49:10.603 --> 00:49:12.340
where I'm trying to think about

00:49:12.340 --> 00:49:15.250
which is almost (mumbles)
get out of the question

00:49:15.250 --> 00:49:17.810
of trying to bracket the
question of the demons

00:49:17.810 --> 00:49:20.623
and whether I can even
understand what they're doing.

00:49:21.490 --> 00:49:24.110
And there's a part of the chapter

00:49:24.110 --> 00:49:27.813
that I wasn't able to
include because of time

00:49:27.813 --> 00:49:32.096
where uncle Mobi passes away,

00:49:32.096 --> 00:49:36.581
but he returns in the form
of dreams and spirits,

00:49:36.581 --> 00:49:38.730
and it's almost like he continues

00:49:38.730 --> 00:49:41.080
to refuse to die even after death.

00:49:41.080 --> 00:49:43.253
And so there's that openness

00:49:43.253 --> 00:49:47.810
that I'm not sure if I
want to allow for closure

00:49:47.810 --> 00:49:50.043
to analyze what's
actually happening there.

00:49:51.120 --> 00:49:52.780
Is uncle Mobi actually coming back

00:49:52.780 --> 00:49:54.300
or is this like an uncanny thing,

00:49:54.300 --> 00:49:56.380
but I think I'm trying to see how

00:49:56.380 --> 00:49:59.870
his death actually created
this report effect.

00:49:59.870 --> 00:50:04.740
And we generate a lot of
discourse around haunting

00:50:04.740 --> 00:50:08.140
that I am not quite sure I should...

00:50:09.210 --> 00:50:13.483
Maybe I should refuse to
theorize it too rigorously.

00:50:14.940 --> 00:50:16.694
So maybe allowing the ethnography

00:50:16.694 --> 00:50:19.530
to haunt itself (mumbles)
I guess, I don't know.

00:50:22.639 --> 00:50:24.130
Thank you for the great question.

00:50:24.130 --> 00:50:25.380
- [Eric] Thank you, Ting.

00:50:26.600 --> 00:50:27.770
Does anyone a question?

00:50:27.770 --> 00:50:29.730
I have several questions, but I'm mindful.

00:50:29.730 --> 00:50:31.960
I wanna make sure other
people have the chance to ask

00:50:31.960 --> 00:50:34.620
but if there's not one at the moment,

00:50:34.620 --> 00:50:38.683
I will ask one first and
then follow up later.

00:50:40.290 --> 00:50:43.410
So Ting this is a fantastic
talk, very interesting.

00:50:43.410 --> 00:50:46.630
There's a lot of directions
to go that I'm interested in,

00:50:46.630 --> 00:50:48.370
but I wanna talk a little bit

00:50:48.370 --> 00:50:52.000
about sort of the micro politics

00:50:52.000 --> 00:50:54.310
and spatial organization

00:50:54.310 --> 00:50:57.550
of these places in the valley

00:50:57.550 --> 00:50:59.500
where you were working in the villages.

00:51:00.950 --> 00:51:05.280
I'm curious if you're
doing this kind of research

00:51:05.280 --> 00:51:09.070
and there's such a kind
of negative stereotype

00:51:09.070 --> 00:51:13.050
of the alcohol drinking Lisu
men among the Christian Lisu.

00:51:14.710 --> 00:51:18.210
When you fall into a group
of men doing field work,

00:51:18.210 --> 00:51:23.060
or families that have men of this nature,

00:51:23.060 --> 00:51:25.042
does that end up
precluding the possibility

00:51:25.042 --> 00:51:27.290
of working with Christians

00:51:27.290 --> 00:51:30.750
and how does that get negotiated?

00:51:30.750 --> 00:51:32.360
And that raised my other question,

00:51:32.360 --> 00:51:34.860
sort of just ethnographic
description a little bit

00:51:36.000 --> 00:51:38.157
about the Christianity in the area

00:51:38.157 --> 00:51:40.780
and the spatial organization of villages,

00:51:40.780 --> 00:51:44.240
are there sort of spatial arrangements

00:51:44.240 --> 00:51:47.580
and where these men
live in a separate part

00:51:47.580 --> 00:51:50.340
or even sort of on the outskirts outside

00:51:50.340 --> 00:51:53.223
or anything like that,
or forms of exclusion?

00:51:54.550 --> 00:51:58.500
In terms of the actual
Christian conversion process

00:51:59.440 --> 00:52:02.330
are people born Christian
or did they convert?

00:52:02.330 --> 00:52:03.870
And the reason I asked that is

00:52:03.870 --> 00:52:06.150
because some of what you're describing,

00:52:06.150 --> 00:52:09.290
when in America 12 step programs

00:52:09.290 --> 00:52:10.914
and all these things
there's a lot of literature

00:52:10.914 --> 00:52:13.990
that shows the parallel to conversion

00:52:13.990 --> 00:52:17.410
and there's a lot of Christian
language in those processes.

00:52:17.410 --> 00:52:18.790
And sometimes when I was listening to you,

00:52:18.790 --> 00:52:19.623
I was thinking, "Well,

00:52:19.623 --> 00:52:21.287
"the way in which some of the Christians

00:52:21.287 --> 00:52:24.267
"are responding to the alcoholism,

00:52:24.267 --> 00:52:27.524
"sounds kind of similar
to like the way a member

00:52:27.524 --> 00:52:30.430
"of a 12 step program might speak of it."

00:52:30.430 --> 00:52:31.784
And so that made me wonder

00:52:31.784 --> 00:52:34.390
if some of the Christians themselves

00:52:34.390 --> 00:52:38.250
had been former drinkers and
this kind of reaction to it

00:52:38.250 --> 00:52:41.900
is based on these kinds of dynamics.

00:52:41.900 --> 00:52:43.030
So there's a lot in there,

00:52:43.030 --> 00:52:44.707
but mainly I want to
hear a little bit more

00:52:44.707 --> 00:52:48.530
about the micro relations of
Christians and non-Christians

00:52:48.530 --> 00:52:51.003
and also how that affected
you as a field worker.

00:52:54.020 --> 00:52:55.270
- [Ting] Thank you, Eric.

00:52:56.210 --> 00:53:01.210
Yeah, the micro politics
were complicated to navigate.

00:53:04.800 --> 00:53:08.310
Actually, I think it
was the other way around

00:53:08.310 --> 00:53:10.630
that when I first started working,

00:53:10.630 --> 00:53:12.393
I worked a lot with Christians.

00:53:15.260 --> 00:53:17.633
My first visit to the Nu River Valley

00:53:17.633 --> 00:53:20.220
I was working with Christian missionaries

00:53:20.220 --> 00:53:22.975
and I felt that during those early visits

00:53:22.975 --> 00:53:27.975
oftentimes I was precluded
talking with non-Christians

00:53:28.020 --> 00:53:29.810
and they were like, "Don't talk to them."

00:53:29.810 --> 00:53:31.907
Or the Christians would move away

00:53:31.907 --> 00:53:34.344
as I will follow the Christians.

00:53:34.344 --> 00:53:36.548
And even though I was
actually very curious

00:53:36.548 --> 00:53:38.673
to talk to the non-Christians.

00:53:40.410 --> 00:53:43.750
At times I think earlier
on, I think, I was biased

00:53:43.750 --> 00:53:47.767
by some Christian perceptions
as well often thinking,

00:53:47.767 --> 00:53:51.963
"Oh, these people, why
don't they not drink?

00:53:54.200 --> 00:53:56.073
I think I took a lot of my own

00:53:56.073 --> 00:53:59.460
having to interrogate
those assumptions as well

00:53:59.460 --> 00:54:01.113
which were the assumptions of the people

00:54:01.113 --> 00:54:04.163
that I was working with at the beginning.

00:54:07.149 --> 00:54:10.167
It took years to build relationships

00:54:11.020 --> 00:54:12.520
with the Christian communities

00:54:14.200 --> 00:54:17.380
and then work with non-Christians

00:54:17.380 --> 00:54:20.210
and still have that trust
with the Christians,

00:54:20.210 --> 00:54:24.406
that I'm not an alcohol drinker.

00:54:24.406 --> 00:54:25.880
I think that was actually...

00:54:25.880 --> 00:54:28.530
This was actually something
I was thinking today that

00:54:30.650 --> 00:54:33.120
it was easier for me to be.

00:54:33.120 --> 00:54:34.530
I wonder how it would have been like

00:54:34.530 --> 00:54:35.750
if I had gone to the field

00:54:35.750 --> 00:54:37.097
and just be really stubborn and say,

00:54:37.097 --> 00:54:39.567
"I drink alcohol and I'm not Christian

00:54:39.567 --> 00:54:43.210
"and I'm just going to hang
out with the non-Christians."

00:54:43.210 --> 00:54:45.540
But I did not, that was
not my starting point.

00:54:45.540 --> 00:54:47.037
The starting point was,

00:54:47.037 --> 00:54:48.397
"I'll go to church,

00:54:48.397 --> 00:54:49.907
"I'll do the rituals with you.

00:54:49.907 --> 00:54:51.187
"I'm not Christian,

00:54:51.187 --> 00:54:54.707
"but I will follow the steps

00:54:54.707 --> 00:54:57.217
"and be included in your conversation

00:54:57.217 --> 00:55:00.507
"and be willing to listen
and learn about the Bible."

00:55:01.360 --> 00:55:05.030
And then that starting
point then allowed me,

00:55:05.030 --> 00:55:07.480
the non-Christians allowed
me to talk to them.

00:55:07.480 --> 00:55:09.940
But I think if it was the other way around

00:55:09.940 --> 00:55:11.460
that if I started as a non Christian,

00:55:11.460 --> 00:55:13.062
I'm not so sure if the
Christian communities

00:55:13.062 --> 00:55:17.263
will be so welcome welcoming to me.

00:55:18.840 --> 00:55:21.140
Because I started with
the Christian communities

00:55:21.140 --> 00:55:23.850
then it was difficult to get out

00:55:23.850 --> 00:55:26.180
into talking to the non-Christians

00:55:26.180 --> 00:55:27.900
because there were lots of gate keeping,

00:55:27.900 --> 00:55:30.945
but the non-Christians were welcoming

00:55:30.945 --> 00:55:34.070
and were willing to talk and ultimately,

00:55:34.070 --> 00:55:38.093
after a lot of kind of
negotiation, it was fine.

00:55:41.750 --> 00:55:44.880
Although I think because I
was living in a household

00:55:44.880 --> 00:55:47.580
where the women were Christian.

00:55:47.580 --> 00:55:51.103
So in that sense, I had
to respect the women

00:55:51.103 --> 00:55:56.103
and also navigate being open
to hanging out with the men,

00:55:56.420 --> 00:56:00.550
but also respecting
auntie for wanting to...

00:56:00.550 --> 00:56:02.160
She wanted to go to church

00:56:02.160 --> 00:56:05.283
and to not drink alcohol with the men.

00:56:06.350 --> 00:56:07.990
The villagers...

00:56:10.040 --> 00:56:11.910
So all these micro politics play out

00:56:11.910 --> 00:56:13.890
in very ritualistic ways as well.

00:56:13.890 --> 00:56:16.145
So even though the
villagers in everyday ways,

00:56:16.145 --> 00:56:19.690
they kind of do get along with each other,

00:56:19.690 --> 00:56:24.180
but they don't eat Christian...

00:56:24.180 --> 00:56:26.650
Christians don't eat the
meat of non-Christians.

00:56:26.650 --> 00:56:31.650
So if the non-Christian had
a funeral or a family member

00:56:31.960 --> 00:56:35.460
and the non-Christians also
had a funeral or a wedding,

00:56:35.460 --> 00:56:40.460
the Christians cannot consume
the meat of the non-Christian.

00:56:40.500 --> 00:56:42.670
So even though in an everyday way,

00:56:42.670 --> 00:56:44.627
people kind of get along with each other

00:56:44.627 --> 00:56:48.328
in the kind of structural
way or individual setting,

00:56:48.328 --> 00:56:51.373
these boundaries are very clearly drawn.

00:56:52.280 --> 00:56:55.163
And in the kind of backstage,

00:56:56.250 --> 00:56:57.690
the conversations do take place

00:56:57.690 --> 00:56:59.740
where the Christians are
critiquing the non-Christians

00:56:59.740 --> 00:57:03.020
and then the non-Christians
like critiquing the Christians.

00:57:03.020 --> 00:57:08.020
I think being with a
Christian friend, auntie Yona,

00:57:09.980 --> 00:57:12.800
I think it was possible
to listen to both sides.

00:57:12.800 --> 00:57:16.990
And so that was auntie Yona and I,

00:57:16.990 --> 00:57:19.150
being open-minded, be with both groups

00:57:19.150 --> 00:57:21.970
and allowing conversations

00:57:21.970 --> 00:57:23.770
to take place in between the groups.

00:57:27.660 --> 00:57:29.270
I can go on about the micro politics

00:57:29.270 --> 00:57:32.700
because it also affects kind
of the village dynamics.

00:57:32.700 --> 00:57:34.600
So the village has a
whole hierarchy system

00:57:34.600 --> 00:57:36.810
and then the men have
their own hierarchy system.

00:57:36.810 --> 00:57:39.210
So when the men were
conducting work parties

00:57:39.210 --> 00:57:41.490
they will follow their own hierarchies

00:57:41.490 --> 00:57:42.734
of who are the seniors

00:57:42.734 --> 00:57:45.630
and the Christians will
follow some other hierarchies.

00:57:45.630 --> 00:57:47.400
And so it's almost like they
have two different bubbles,

00:57:47.400 --> 00:57:48.850
but intersecting.

00:57:48.850 --> 00:57:50.523
The spatial arrangements.

00:57:51.910 --> 00:57:52.920
Yeah, that's really interesting

00:57:52.920 --> 00:57:53.753
'cause I did not think about it,

00:57:53.753 --> 00:57:55.360
but in fact it is so true

00:57:55.360 --> 00:57:59.090
that the non-Christians
tend to be living away

00:57:59.090 --> 00:58:00.993
from the Christians, further out.

00:58:03.136 --> 00:58:05.105
Where the non-Christians were living,

00:58:05.105 --> 00:58:09.380
the people tend to consider
they were near haunted sites.

00:58:09.380 --> 00:58:12.680
I did not consider that
was connected directly

00:58:12.680 --> 00:58:15.120
to alcohol drinking,

00:58:15.120 --> 00:58:18.493
but I think there is
some sort of connection.

00:58:19.944 --> 00:58:23.780
The people often consider
the pathways that go

00:58:23.780 --> 00:58:25.540
towards an non-Christians household

00:58:25.540 --> 00:58:27.250
and there will be a haunted site,

00:58:27.250 --> 00:58:31.270
or there's a kind of
tombs around the places

00:58:31.270 --> 00:58:33.930
where the non-Christians reside.

00:58:33.930 --> 00:58:36.880
But this whole arrangement
now is, I understand,

00:58:36.880 --> 00:58:40.480
is completely changed since
I was last able to visit

00:58:40.480 --> 00:58:43.233
because of the One Belt
One Road Initiative

00:58:43.233 --> 00:58:44.990
which runs through the village.

00:58:44.990 --> 00:58:47.070
And so all the villages
who are living by the roads

00:58:47.070 --> 00:58:51.390
have had to move up into the
villages, into the mountains.

00:58:51.390 --> 00:58:53.630
I wasn't working deep into the mountains,

00:58:53.630 --> 00:58:55.030
but somewhere in the middle.

00:58:56.305 --> 00:58:59.740
Now, all the spatial
arrangements are messed up.

00:58:59.740 --> 00:59:02.120
And auntie Yona was saying,
next time I come back

00:59:02.120 --> 00:59:04.320
I won't be able to
recognize people's houses

00:59:04.320 --> 00:59:06.960
because everybody's
living next to each other.

00:59:06.960 --> 00:59:09.330
It's very crowded now,
this is what I sense.

00:59:09.330 --> 00:59:12.181
And the 12 step thing is so
accurate, that's so great.

00:59:12.181 --> 00:59:13.014
Thank you.

00:59:13.014 --> 00:59:16.650
Yes. A lot of the men
were alcohol drinkers

00:59:16.650 --> 00:59:18.900
and in that sense, there's
the haunting there as well

00:59:18.900 --> 00:59:21.161
because they're haunted
by (mumbles) higher.

00:59:21.161 --> 00:59:23.505
And so there's a very strong kind of,

00:59:23.505 --> 00:59:26.250
"We're going to reject
the alcohol drinkers."

00:59:26.250 --> 00:59:29.090
But most of the villagers
were alcohol drinkers,

00:59:29.090 --> 00:59:31.650
and it's only in the last 20 years,

00:59:31.650 --> 00:59:34.350
that this Christianity has picked up

00:59:34.350 --> 00:59:35.833
in this particular village.

00:59:39.260 --> 00:59:40.850
